## Why This Is Asked
Tests non-branching bit-twiddling and packing/unpacking across a mask, a common performance pattern in high-throughput code.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and order-preserving packing
- Non-branching compression via cascade masks
- Fixed-sequence bit-manipulation tricks

## Code Example
```javascript
function bitpack64(v, mask){
  let x = v & mask;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & 0x5555555555555555n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & 0x3333333333333333n;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 8n)) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 16n)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFn;
  x = (x ^ (x >> 32n)) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFn;
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic masks without recompilation?
- Compare performance with a loop-based approach on large data sets.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of fast, branchless bit tricks and constant-time bit-scanning utilities common in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- Isolating least-significant 1: v = n & -n
- De Bruijn-based indexing: map a 5-bit index to a trailing-zero count
- Handling edge case n == 0 (return 32)
- Static lookup table of 32 entries

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t countTrailingZeros32(uint32_t n){
  static const int debruijn[32] = {0,1,28,2,29,14,24,3,30,22,20,15,25,17,4,8,31,27,13,23,21,19,16,7,26,12,18,9,6,11,5,10};
  if(n == 0) return 32;
  uint32_t v = n & -n;
  return debruijn[(v * 0x077CB531) >> 27];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- What are alternatives that avoid a static table, e.g., using binary search on bit groups?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-boundary bit tricks and 128-bit reasoning without loops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit rotation via 64-bit halves\n- Normalizing k to 0..127, masking to 128 bits\n- Pure bitwise/shifts, no branches\n\n## Code Example
```javascript
function rotl128(hi, lo, k) {
  const n = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const kk = BigInt(k) & 127n;
  const mask128 = (1n << 128n) - 1n;
  const val = ((n << kk) | (n >> (128n - kk))) & mask128;
  return {
    hi: Number((val >> 64n) & ((1n << 64n) - 1n)),
    lo: Number(val & ((1n << 64n) - 1n))
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement without 128-bit types?\n- How to test edge cases: k=0, k=127, k=64?\n- How to port to languages with limited integer width or without unsigned128 support?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep bit-slicing, 128-bit composition, and Morton (Z-order) interleaving in a branchless, portable way.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order interleaving
- Bit-slicing with masks and shifts (part1by1 technique)
- 64-bit to 128-bit composition without branches
- Endianness implications for MSB-first construction

## Code Example
```c
struct uint128_t {
    uint64_t hi, lo;
};

// Spread single bits to double-bit positions
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x ^ (x << 16)) & 0x0000ffff0000ffff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 8))  & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ff;
    x = (x ^ (x << 4))  & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
    x = (x ^ (x << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333;
    x = (x ^ (x << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555;
    return x;
}

struct uint128_t interleave64(uint64_t a, uint64_t b) {
    uint64_t a_spread = part1by1(a);
    uint64_t b_spread = part1by1(b);

    struct uint128_t result;
    result.lo = (a_spread << 1) | b_spread;
    result.hi = (a_spread >> 63) | (b_spread >> 63);
    return result;
}
```

This implementation uses the classic part1by1 technique to spread bits, then combines them with simple shifts and OR operations, achieving the required bit alternation without loops or conditionals.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness understanding and safe 128-bit lane manipulation with pure bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Byte reversal with masks
- 128-bit struct arithmetic
- Endianness swapping

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo);
  r.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a compile-time constant shift? 
- How to verify with unit tests across edge cases (all zeros, all ones, cross-boundary values)?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to perform a branchless, cross-boundary bitwise scan on a 128-bit value—a common primitive in cryptography and systems programming. It verifies you can correctly propagate carries and parity across the 64-bit lane boundary without branching.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel prefix (scan) technique**: Each shift-XOR step doubles the bit-range being folded, achieving O(log n) instead of O(n) operations.
- **128-bit right shift implementation**: Requires explicit carry propagation between the hi and lo 64-bit words.
- **Branchless design**: No conditionals ensures constant-time execution, critical for crypto applications.

## Code Example

```javascript
function prefixXor128(x_lo, x_hi) {
  let lo = x_lo, hi = x_hi;
  // 128-bit shift helper: (lo, hi) >> k
  const shift128 = (lo, hi, k) => {
    if (k < 64) return { lo: (lo >>> k) | ((hi & ((1n << k) - 1n)) << (64 - k)), hi: hi >>> k };
    return { lo: hi >>> (k - 64), hi: 0 };
  };
  // Parallel prefix: x ^= x >> {1,2,4,8,16,32,64}
  for (const k of [1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]) {
    const s = shift128(lo, hi, k);
    lo ^= s.lo; hi ^= s.hi;
  }
  return { lo, hi };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a prefix OR or prefix AND?
- What changes are needed to handle SIMD/vector implementations?
- How does this compare to a naive loop-based approach in terms of cycle count?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply SWAR bit counting to 128-bit data without branches. Also checks splitting 128-bit into halves and combining results efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR bitcount technique for 64-bit words
- Handling 128-bit data via hi/lo halves
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t v) {
  v = v - ((v >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555);
  v = (v & 0x3333333333333333) + ((v >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333);
  v = (v + (v >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0f;
  return (v * 0x0101010101010101) >> 56;
}
```
Then: hiCount = popcount64(x.hi); loCount = popcount64(x.lo); return hiCount + loCount;

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this extend to 256-bit values?
- Compare with using hardware intrinsics (e.g., PDEP/POPCNT) on supporting CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless thinking and lane-wise masking, fundamental techniques in low-level libraries and SIMD operations.

## Key Concepts
- Lane-wise masking without conditionals using two's complement arithmetic
- 128-bit composition from 32-bit chunks
- Bitwise extraction and packing operations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode C-like implementation demonstrating the core idea
uint128 blendMask128(uint128 x){
  uint32_t a3 = (x.hi >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a2 = x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a1 = (x.lo >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint32_t a0 = x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF;
  uint64_t m3 = -(uint64_t)(a3 & 1);
  uint64_t m2 = -(uint64_t)(a2 & 1);
  uint64_t m1 = -(uint64_t)(a1 & 1);
  uint64_t m0 = -(uint64_t)(a0 & 1);
  uint128 result;
  result.hi = (m3 << 32) | m2;
  result.lo = (m1 << 32) | m0;
  return result;
}
```

## Quick Tests
- Input: 0x123456789ABCDEF0 → Output: 0x0000000000000000 (all lanes even)
- Input: 0x12345679_9ABCDEF1 → Output: 0xFFFFFFFF_00000000 (only A0 odd)

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of non-trivial bit interleaving without loops, a common low-level optimization in graphics/encoding.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (Morton order)
- Mask-and-shift interleaving
- 128-bit composition from two 64-bit halves

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudocode outline (no branches)
uint128 interleaveBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  uint64_t sLo = spreadBy2(lo); // using masks 0x5555.., 0x3333.., etc.
  uint64_t sHi = spreadBy2(hi);
  r.lo = sLo;
  r.hi = sHi << 1;
  return r;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo, avoiding loops, and applying a scalable, branchless popcount.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo
- SWAR/parallel bit counting
- Endianness and safe casting between types

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x -= (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (uint8_t)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to accumulate using wider accumulators? 
- What is the performance impact on platforms with native popcount intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
This asks for deep bit-manipulation skill: a palindrome across 128 bits is a nontrivial symmetry that can be checked in constant time with a reversal network, not with loops. It reveals understanding of endian-ness and 128-bit layout.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit word as {uint64 hi, lo}
- Bit-reversal networks using masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F..., etc.
- Palindrome property across all 128 bits
- Edge-case tests (all zeros, ends-set pattern)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
static inline uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.lo = reverse64(x.hi); r.hi = reverse64(x.lo); return r;
}
int isPalindromicBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r = reverse128(x);
  return (x.hi == r.hi) && (x.lo == r.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support streaming 128-bit palindromicity with partial data?
- What are the performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit targets?
